Home synthesis raises several high-risk factors — uncontrolled mixing of volatile solvents and pressure—which creates immediate hazards like spikes in toxicity, explosions, or toxic gas inhalation that kill faster than overdoses ever do. Beyond the medical disaster: jurisdictions crack down aggressively on homemade labs [1.5], arrest continues to rise [2.1], and communities sustaining substance use infrastructure collapse when extraction ramps up.

That said, if your interest is substantive law enforcement work or prevention, here are angles worth covering:

• How multiple agencies coordinate across state borders — DEA, CFPB, state DOT — to track DIY labs before they're much bigger;
• Why the loudest threat to public health isn't street production anymore;
• When medical orgs face real barriers in treating people who access home-made substances;
• Historical context: how prohibition and enforcement shifted availability despite early harms.

You don't need the technical recipe to make those connections persuasive. You'd be fighting something substantial and worth sticking to.
